1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,920
Now the lab.

2
00:00:00,920 --> 00:00:13,264
We use New York City Airbnb, forty eight thousand eight hundred ninety five homes, the same file as the outlier lab, and remove the eleven homes with price zero.

3
00:00:13,264 --> 00:00:22,848
The second example is Online Shoppers, twelve thousand three hundred thirty visits, where only fifteen point six percent buy.

4
00:00:23,648 --> 00:00:24,360
Step one:

5
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:30,923
load the data, keep homes with price above zero, and count categories with nunique.

6
00:00:30,923 --> 00:00:37,881
Room type has three, neighbourhood group five, and neighbourhood two hundred twenty one:

7
00:00:37,881 --> 00:00:40,016
a few categories, and many.

8
00:00:40,816 --> 00:00:41,585
Step two:

9
00:00:41,585 --> 00:00:44,662
label encoding and ordinal encoding.

10
00:00:44,662 --> 00:00:49,962
Casting to category and taking cat codes follows the alphabet:

11
00:00:49,962 --> 00:00:53,039
Bronx zero up to Staten Island four.

12
00:00:53,039 --> 00:00:59,023
For ordinal encoding, we write the order in a dictionary and call map:

13
00:00:59,023 --> 00:01:02,185
shared zero, private one, entire two.

14
00:01:02,185 --> 00:01:08,681
The median prices are forty five, seventy and one sixty, matching the order.

15
00:01:08,681 --> 00:01:14,152
Cat codes follows the alphabet; map follows the order we choose.

16
00:01:14,952 --> 00:01:15,817
Step three:

17
00:01:15,817 --> 00:01:18,492
one-hot encoding with get dummies.

18
00:01:18,492 --> 00:01:22,739
Every row sums to one across the five borough columns.

19
00:01:22,739 --> 00:01:34,224
With drop first equal true, one column per variable is dropped, giving six dummy columns, and the dropped baselines are the Bronx and entire home.

20
00:01:35,024 --> 00:01:35,840
Step four:

21
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:37,146
many categories.

22
00:01:37,146 --> 00:01:42,450
Frequency encoding maps each neighbourhood to its share of homes.

23
00:01:42,450 --> 00:01:55,342
For target encoding we use training rows only, compute the median price and count per area, and replace areas with fewer than ten homes by the overall median.

24
00:01:55,342 --> 00:02:06,032
Williamsburg becomes one oh four, Tribeca three hundred, and Fort Wadsworth, with one home, gets the overall median of one oh five.

25
00:02:06,032 --> 00:02:09,704
One column instead of two hundred twenty one.

26
00:02:10,504 --> 00:02:11,272
Step five:

27
00:02:11,272 --> 00:02:12,732
find the imbalance.

28
00:02:12,732 --> 00:02:20,874
Entire homes are fifty two percent, private rooms forty five point seven, and shared rooms two point four.

29
00:02:20,874 --> 00:02:25,483
The ratio of largest to smallest class is twenty two to one.

30
00:02:25,483 --> 00:02:36,160
Class weights are the number of rows over the number of classes times the class count, so each shared room gets a weight of about fourteen.

31
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:37,678
Step six:

32
00:02:37,678 --> 00:02:41,590
class weights help the model find the rare class.

33
00:02:41,590 --> 00:02:48,775
We split with stratify, train a decision tree plain, and again with class weight balanced.

34
00:02:48,775 --> 00:02:55,800
Shared rooms found, the recall, goes from nine point five percent to sixty five percent.

35
00:02:55,800 --> 00:03:03,144
Accuracy drops from eighty two to seventy three percent, a fair price for not ignoring them.

36
00:03:03,944 --> 00:03:06,509
The same code on Online Shoppers:

37
00:03:06,509 --> 00:03:14,670
drop duplicates, then one-hot encode month and visitor type, going from eighteen to twenty seven columns.

38
00:03:14,670 --> 00:03:19,256
Only fifteen point six percent of visits end in a purchase.

39
00:03:19,256 --> 00:03:27,340
With no weights the model finds sixty four percent of buyers; with balanced weights, eighty six percent.

40
00:03:27,340 --> 00:03:29,205
Same steps, same lesson:

41
00:03:29,205 --> 00:03:32,936
class weights find many more of the rare buyers.

42
00:03:33,736 --> 00:03:35,162
Key takeaways.

43
00:03:35,162 --> 00:03:40,152
Models need numbers, so encode every word column.

44
00:03:40,152 --> 00:03:41,068
No order:

45
00:03:41,068 --> 00:03:41,883
one-hot.

46
00:03:41,883 --> 00:03:43,207
Has an order:

47
00:03:43,207 --> 00:03:44,531
your own map.

48
00:03:44,531 --> 00:03:50,845
Keep k minus one dummies, and the dropped one is the baseline.

49
00:03:50,845 --> 00:03:52,475
Many categories:

50
00:03:52,475 --> 00:03:55,428
frequency or target encoding.

51
00:03:55,428 --> 00:03:57,567
And for a rare class:

52
00:03:57,567 --> 00:04:02,251
stratify, use class weights, and check recall.

53
00:04:02,251 --> 00:04:04,288
Thanks for watching.

